import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Exporting","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"EXPORTING.md","filePath":"EXPORTING.md"}'),n={name:"EXPORTING.md"};function r(i,e,d,h,l,c){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="exporting" tabindex="-1">Exporting <a class="header-anchor" href="#exporting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exporting&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Besides playing a song in real time, llamatron writes a song out: as a Live set to finish in a DAW, as MIDI files for any other DAW or player, or as a WAV file to listen to anywhere. This page covers every kind of export, where the files go, and exporting from the command line; <a href="./GETTING_STARTED#opening-and-saving">Opening and Saving</a> covers saving the set itself.</p><h2 id="tracker-export" tabindex="-1">Tracker Export <a class="header-anchor" href="#tracker-export" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tracker Export&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>Ctrl+X</code>, on any page, exports the current song and asks which kind first: Ableton Live set, <code>MIDI …</code> or WAV (FluidSynth). <code>MIDI …</code> opens a small window of its four kinds over the list, where <code>ENTER</code> exports the one chosen and <code>ESC</code> goes back to the list. A set that hasn&#39;t been saved yet is saved first, since an export is named after the set&#39;s file and written beside it. When the export is written, the status line says where it went.</p><p>Every export is named <code>&lt;set&gt;_&lt;song&gt;</code>, so <code>songs/foo.yml</code> with a song called <code>bar</code> exports as:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>File</th><th>What it&#39;s for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ableton Live set</td><td><code>songs/foo_bar.als</code></td><td>The main one: each track a MIDI track, its clips in Session View and the scenes laid end to end in Arrangement View, ready for instruments, effects and mixing.</td></tr><tr><td>MIDI (Stems)</td><td><code>songs/foo_bar_stem_001-lead.mid</code> and so on</td><td>A file per track, each the full length of the song with the tempo changes, for hardware and samplers that take one file at a time.</td></tr><tr><td>MIDI (Multitrack)</td><td><code>songs/foo_bar.mid</code></td><td>One file with a track per track and the tempo changes, which most DAWs import as aligned tracks in one drop. Every track is on channel 1 with no program change, so that it plays whatever instrument the DAW gives it. For MuseScore, GarageBand or another player with General MIDI&#39;s sounds, choose MIDI (General MIDI).</td></tr><tr><td>MIDI (Clips)</td><td><code>songs/foo_bar_clip_s_002-chorus-t_001-lead.mid</code> and so on</td><td>A file per clip, named by scene and track, to drag into a project one by one.</td></tr><tr><td>MIDI (General MIDI)</td><td><code>songs/foo_bar_gm.mid</code></td><td>One file for any General MIDI player, GarageBand or a phone: drums on channel 10, every other track on a channel of its own, each with its instrument&#39;s GM sound and, where set, its Pan.</td></tr><tr><td>WAV (FluidSynth)</td><td><code>songs/foo_bar.wav</code></td><td>The song as audio, for anyone with no DAW: the tracks whose instrument&#39;s Device is <code>*FluidSynth</code>, played by FluidSynth faster than real time and normalized to a peak 1 dB below full scale. Choosing it says first that only those tracks are in it, and it needs FluidSynth installed.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="export-contents" tabindex="-1">Export Contents <a class="header-anchor" href="#export-contents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Export Contents&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An export plays the song as written, every scene once through from the top, as Live&#39;s Arrangement View lays it out, with every generator, groove, draw and random choice played into the notes. Every export is a new take of the song&#39;s random choices.</p><p>Some things belong to real-time playback alone and no export reads them: instruments&#39; devices, a clip&#39;s follow action and a scene&#39;s Launch together, the Live response, and anything you did by hand while the song played. Muted tracks are left out of the MIDI and WAV exports, and in the Live set they are switched off. The MIDI files lay the scenes end to end as Live&#39;s Arrangement View does, so a track that plays nothing in the first scenes starts with silence and every file lines up. Every MIDI file carries the song&#39;s time signature, and so does the Live set, so a DAW draws its bar lines on the song&#39;s beats. The MIDI file, the MIDI stems, the MIDI clips and the Live set carry the keyswitch notes described in <a href="./INSTRUMENTS">Instruments</a>; the General MIDI file and the WAV file leave them out.</p><p>A scene&#39;s <a href="./SCENES#tempo-ramps">tempo ramps</a> are written into every MIDI file and the WAV file, a tempo change at each step. In a Live set, the ramps are tempo automation in Arrangement View, which follows them. A scene in Live&#39;s Session View launches at one tempo and cannot ramp, so each scene there launches at the tempo of its first beat. To hear the ramps while launching scenes, play the song from llamatron into Live over MIDI in real time, or import the MIDI file.</p><h2 id="file-locations" tabindex="-1">File Locations <a class="header-anchor" href="#file-locations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;File Locations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Exports sit beside the set&#39;s file, so a set and its exports stay together wherever you run llamatron from, and every file of a song starts with <code>&lt;set&gt;_&lt;song&gt;</code>, so exporting one song never touches another&#39;s.</p><p>Writing a Live set over an earlier one asks first, once in a session for each set, because replacing it loses anything you saved over that set in Live; to keep work you&#39;ve done in Live, save it there under another name. The MIDI kinds and the WAV just write over their earlier files. The exports of the sets in <code>examples/</code> are ignored by git.</p><p>In Live, each llamatron track is a MIDI track with no instrument, so drop one onto each track to hear it. Scenes keep their names, and each clip is named after the pattern it plays. The project was made with Ableton Live 12.3, and later versions should open it.</p><h2 id="command-line-export" tabindex="-1">Command-Line Export <a class="header-anchor" href="#command-line-export" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Command-Line Export&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>--to</code> exports a set without opening the tracker, writing every song in it beside the set&#39;s file, the same as <code>Ctrl+X</code>. On its own it writes Live sets, and it can name another kind: <code>ableton</code>, <code>midi</code> (MIDI (Multitrack)), <code>stems</code>, <code>cliptree</code> (MIDI (Clips)), <code>gm</code> (MIDI (General MIDI)) or <code>wav</code> (WAV (FluidSynth)). Put it after the set&#39;s name:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>./llamatron.sh examples/by_genre/electronic/minimal_techno.yml --to</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>./llamatron.sh examples/by_genre/electronic/minimal_techno.yml --to midi</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>./llamatron.sh examples/by_genre/electronic/minimal_techno.yml --to wav --out exports</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>./llamatron.sh examples/by_genre/electronic/minimal_techno.yml --to ableton --replace</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>./llamatron.sh examples/learning/04_scale.yml --to --song scale</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>--song</code></td><td>Exports only the song with that name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>--out</code></td><td>Writes into that folder, with the same file names, in place of the set&#39;s own folder.</td></tr><tr><td><code>--replace</code></td><td>Removes each song&#39;s earlier export first, so you can export again and listen.</td></tr><tr><td><code>--template</code></td><td>Builds the Ableton Live set from another Live set, such as one with your own devices and routing, in place of the one installed with llamatron.</td></tr><tr><td><code>--force</code></td><td>Exports a set that holds values that cannot be used, each changed to the nearest allowed value and listed; the set file is not changed. Without it, such a set is refused, with each change it would make listed.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These only work with <code>--to</code>.</p><h2 id="batch-export-scripts" tabindex="-1">Batch Export Scripts <a class="header-anchor" href="#batch-export-scripts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Batch Export Scripts&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Script</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sh user_scripts/demos_to_live_sets.sh</code></td><td>Every example as a Live set beside it, replacing earlier exports.</td></tr><tr><td><code>sh user_scripts/demos_to_midi.sh</code></td><td>Every example as one General MIDI file in <code>output/previews/</code>, for any MIDI player.</td></tr><tr><td><code>sh user_scripts/demos_to_wavs.sh</code></td><td>Those previews as WAV files through FluidSynth, installing it the first time.</td></tr></tbody></table>`,21)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,g as default};
